{
  "dom_id": "step-q126065",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "$\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯=$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the given sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯= \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{(1/5)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(1/5)^{3}}{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1/5)^{n}}{n}+⋯ & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\,+\\,\\frac{1}{5}) & =(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{(1/5)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(1/5)^{3}}{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1/5)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{25\\cdot 2}+\\frac{1}{125\\cdot 3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1/5)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{50}+\\frac{1}{375}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n{5}^{n}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}).$",
